Market Challenges
High Development Costs and Manufacturing Complexity
Peptide drug development requires specialized synthesis, purification, analytical testing, and quality-control capabilities. Maintaining consistent product characteristics during scale-up can be challenging, particularly for complex peptide sequences and conjugated products. These requirements increase development costs and can create barriers for smaller biotechnology companies.

Regulatory and Intellectual Property Requirements
Regulatory agencies require detailed characterization of peptide products, including impurity profiles, stability, and manufacturing consistency. Companies must also navigate patent protection across peptide sequences, formulations, production processes, and therapeutic uses. Overlapping intellectual property claims can increase legal and commercialization costs.

Market Restraints
Pharmacokinetic Limitations
Many peptide drugs face limitations involving short plasma half-lives, enzymatic degradation, and poor oral bioavailability. These characteristics can require injectable administration or frequent dosing, potentially affecting patient convenience and treatment adherence. Formulation strategies such as pegylation and depot delivery are being explored to improve drug exposure.

Challenges in Tumor Delivery
Delivering sufficient therapeutic concentrations to certain tumor sites remains difficult. The blood-brain barrier is a particular obstacle for peptide candidates targeting central nervous system malignancies. Differences in tumor biology and patient response can also complicate clinical development and treatment standardization.

